  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $a=\\sqrt{6}$. Find a polynomial with integer coefficients for which $a$ is a root.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=x^2-6$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=6\\\\a^2-6&=0\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $P(x)=x^2-6$ has integer coefficients and $P(a)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt{6}$ is irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{6}$ is irrational.\n\nBy the Integer Root Theorem, the only possible rational roots of $x^2-6$ are $\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,\\pm6$; none is a root.",
          "worked": "$\\sqrt{6}$ is a root of the monic polynomial $P(x)=x^2-6$, whose coefficients are integers. By the Integer Root Theorem, any rational root must be a divisor of $6$, so the only possibilities are $\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,\\pm6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\pm1)&=1-6=-5\\ne0\\\\P(\\pm2)&=4-6=-2\\ne0\\\\P(\\pm3)&=9-6=3\\ne0\\\\P(\\pm6)&=36-6=30\\ne0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $P(x)$ has no rational roots. Since $P(\\sqrt{6})=0$, $\\sqrt{6}$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
  },
